==== How to Fly: The Glass-Cockpit Cross-Country

A complete cross-country leg in the SR22 G1000, step by step. The screens do the heavy lifting; you do the thinking. Exact page labels and button names follow the aircraft's own manual — the flow is what matters.

===== The Preflight & Plan

1. *Build the leg in LittleNavMap* — departure, destination, route altitudes, fuel stop. Check the METARs and TAFs at both ends; pick the alternate.
2. *Export and load the plan* — save it in the sim's flight-plan format and load it into the aircraft's avionics so the map and the autopilot see the same route you planned.
3. *Walk the numbers* — fuel on board vs trip fuel plus reserve; weather within VFR minimums; the plan is legal before the engine starts.

===== Start & Program

1. *Master on, then start* — follow the SR22's start flow (battery, avionics, prime, start). The G1000 boots through its self-test; let it finish.
2. *Program the boxes* — enter the departure, destination, and route in the flight plan page; set the baro/altimeter from the local weather; set the heading and altitude bugs.
3. *Verify against the map* — the MFD's moving map should match the LittleNavMap plan leg for leg. It is cheaper to catch a mismatch on the ground.

===== Takeoff & Climb

1. *Line up and go* — full power, rotate at the SR22's typical speed, climb at the book's best-rate speed.
2. *Engage the autopilot* — once trimmed and climbing: altitude select for the cruise altitude, heading select for the departure turn, then navigation tracking once you are on the first leg.
3. *Set and forget — no, set and check* — the autopilot holds what you told it; glance at the map and the groundspeed to confirm you are actually going where the plan thinks you are.

===== Cruise on the Autopilot

1. *Lean and level* — in cruise, manage power and mixture for the book's cruise settings; the displays show you fuel flow and endurance — know your numbers before you need them.
2. *Watch the weather* — the moving map and the live picture: if the destination sours, the alternate from your preflight is already known. Decide early, divert calmly.
3. *Time warp* — long legs are the sim's gift: speed up the cruise, slow down for the work. Just remember where you are when you come back.

===== Descent & Arrival

1. *Plan the descent* — start down in time to arrive at the destination's pattern altitude/approach altitude: altitude to lose, divided by your descent rate, plus a margin. The math is simple and saves the whole arrival.
2. *Brief the approach* — pull the plate up in AviTab: runway, altitude, frequency, minimums. Say it out loud — the habit is the skill.
3. *Configure* — power back, flaps as the flow demands, speed for the approach. Let the autopilot or your hand fly the final course; you manage the energy.

===== Approach & Landing

1. *Line up the aim point* — the runway threshold stays fixed in the windshield; adjust power for the glide, pitch for the speed.
2. *The round-out* — over the threshold, flare gently and let the mains settle. A crosswind leg means a crab on final and a kick at the flare — practice it, it is the SR22's only secret.
3. *Rollout & clear* — brakes to taxi speed, exit at the taxiway, and the leg is yours.

===== The Debrief

1. *Replay the landing* — the sim's replay shows what your hands actually did; a rough touchdown usually has a visible cause (speed, flare, or looking at the runway instead of the horizon).
2. *Check the track* — LittleNavMap kept the log: how did the flown track compare to the plan? Where did you drift?
3. *Log it* — every leg is a data point. Wave 2's IFR work is built on the habit of reviewing what you flew.
